  "id": "DrugBank_DB04572 - MONDO_17327",
  "verbalization": "Thiotepa, a chemotherapy agent, is implicated in the treatment of various ovarian tumors through a mechanistic pathway involving the drug's interaction with alternative chemotherapy agents like Altretamine. The evidence suggests that Thiotepa may be linked to the development or progression of Primary Non-gestational Choriocarcinoma Of Ovary through a shared pathway involving Altretamine. This is supported by the presence of a direct indication chain from Thiotepa to Altretamine, which in turn is indicated to target the same disease entity. The final scores for the paths indicate a moderate level of confidence in this relationship, suggesting that the observed indications may reflect a shared biological mechanism or therapeutic context.\n\nThe recurring involvement of Altretamine in the indication chain implies a potential role in the pathogenesis of the disease, possibly through a common pathway or a shared target. The presence of multiple paths with similar scores suggests that the relationship between Thiotepa and the disease is not isolated but may be part of a broader network of chemotherapeutic agents targeting similar molecular pathways. The integration of these paths supports the hypothesis that Thiotepa may exert a therapeutic effect on Primary Non-gestational Choriocarcinoma Of Ovary through a mechanism that involves the interaction with Altretamine, though the exact nature of this mechanism remains to be elucidated.",
